"id": "spelling",
"el": "Ορθογραφία και στίξη",
"title": "Spelling, punctuation and abbreviations",
"span": "A2–B2",
"blurb": "How grammar decides spelling (θα λύσει but η λύση, οι καλοί but η καλή), the sound-alikes πως/πώς, που/πού, η/οι/ή, ότι/ό,τι, πια/πιο, the spelling of common endings (-ώνω, -εύω, -ίζω, -ικός, -εια), stress marks on capitals and the diaeresis, Greek punctuation (; · « » —), capital letters, and the abbreviations and number formats of everyday texts.",
"intro": [
{
"p": "Greek has six ways to write the sound **i** (ι, η, υ, ει, οι, υι) and two for **o** (ο, ω). That looks frightening, but most of the time **grammar** tells you which one to use: the ending of a verb, a noun or an adjective has a fixed spelling. Learn the endings and you can spell words you have only heard. The B2 writing paper takes marks off for spelling and punctuation, so this chapter is worth the time."
},
